--TEST--
`Core\Sse->retry` takes a `Core\Time\Duration` and the wire takes milliseconds, so the unit is said once at the call site and never carried in the number — `rule:concurrency/two-doors-one-isolate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A parameter taking a number of milliseconds would put the unit at every call
// site, where a `Duration` says which unit it is where it is written. What
// reaches the wire is the same field whichever member built the wait.
var $units = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
    $sse->retry(Core\Time\Duration::seconds(3));
    $sse->retry(Core\Time\Duration::milliseconds(250));
    $sse->retry(Core\Time\Duration::minutes(2));
});
echo $units;
echo "--\n";

// A wait shorter than the wire can spell arrives as `0` rather than as a
// refusal: a millisecond is the client's floor, and a program asking for less
// than the wire can say is asking for the least it can say.
var $floor = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
    $sse->retry(Core\Time\Duration::microseconds(10));
});
echo $floor;
echo "--\n";
--EXPECT--
retry: 3000

retry: 250

retry: 120000

--
retry: 0

--
